<BaseLayout title="Conveyor Cost &#x26; Pricing Guide | PackagingConveyor.com" description="What drives conveyor cost: type, size, frame material, controls, sanitation rating, installation, and guarding. Cost-driver table, quote checklist, and questions to ask." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema, faqSchema]}>
<h1>Conveyor Cost &amp; Pricing Guide</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Conveyor cost is set by a handful of drivers: conveyor type, length and width, frame material, motors and drives, controls and accumulation, sanitation or environment rating, installation and integration, and guarding. <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">Gravity roller conveyors</a> sit at the low end. Automated lines with PLC controls, servo indexing, and sanitary stainless construction sit at the high end. This guide explains what pushes each driver up, what to ask the supplier about it, and how to write a quote request that lets you compare bids on the same scope.</p>
<div class="usa-summary-box">
<div class="usa-summary-box__body">
<div class="usa-summary-box__text">
<h2 id="disclaimer-heading" class="usa-summary-box__heading">Why This Guide Uses Cost Drivers, Not Prices</h2>
<p>Conveyor prices move with steel, motors, labor, and region, and a price without the scope behind it misleads more than it helps. This guide does not publish prices or percentages. It explains what makes a project cost more or less, so you can read quotes critically and ask the right questions. For a budget number, send the same written specification to several suppliers and compare their quotes line by line.</p>
</div></div></div>
<h2 id="drivers-heading">Cost Drivers at a Glance</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Cost driver</th><th>What raises cost</th><th>What to ask the supplier</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Conveyor type</td><td>Powered over gravity. Zone-controlled over continuous. Servo indexing, spirals, right-angle transfers, and heavy pallet handling over simple transport.</td><td>Which conveyor type do you propose for my product and rate, and what simpler type did you rule out and why?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Length, width, elevation</td><td>More length, wider beds, heavier frames, curves, inclines, and supports for elevated runs.</td><td>What is the quoted length, between-frame width, and top-of-belt or top-of-roller height? Are supports, curves, and incline sections included?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Frame material</td><td>Aluminum over painted carbon steel. Stainless over both. 316 over 304 stainless. Welded custom frames over bolt-together standard sections.</td><td>What frame material and stainless grade are quoted? What finish? Which parts are not stainless (bearings, fasteners, motor)?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Controls and accumulation</td><td>PLC and HMI over relay logic. Zero-pressure accumulation, many zones, many sensors, VFDs, servo axes, machine integration, and data reporting.</td><td>Exactly what does "controls" include: starters only, or PLC, HMI, sensors, VFDs, panel, wiring, programming? How many accumulation zones and sensors?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sanitation or environment rating</td><td>Washdown, sanitary open-frame design, cleanroom construction, freezer duty, or hazardous-location (explosion-proof) ratings.</td><td>What sanitation standard or design guideline is the conveyor built to? What IP rating are motors and sensors? Which belt materials are rated for food contact?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Installation and integration</td><td>Rigging, overhead or mezzanine work, long electrical runs, removal of old equipment, night or weekend work, and integration with other machines.</td><td>Is installation mechanical only, or electrical and controls commissioning too? Who integrates with upstream and downstream machines? How many on-site commissioning days are included?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Guarding and safety</td><td>Full drive and nip point guarding, interlocked access doors, pull-cord e-stops, light curtains, and safety-rated controls.</td><td>What guarding is included? Which standard is the design built to (for example ASME B20.1)? Are e-stops, pull cords, and interlocks in the quote or listed as options?</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The sections below explain each driver in more detail.</p>
<h2 id="type-heading">Conveyor Type</h2>
<p>Conveyor type is usually the largest cost determinant. A gravity roller section costs a fraction of a servo-driven <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing conveyor</a> of the same length. The table lists common packaging conveyor types roughly from simplest to most complex, with what pushes each one up.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Conveyor type</th><th>What raises cost</th><th>Common applications</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">Gravity roller</a></td><td>Curves, gates, wider widths, closer roller centers</td><td>End-of-line, shipping, packing stations</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flat belt (basic)</td><td>Belt material, width, food-grade belting</td><td>Lightweight product transport</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tabletop chain</td><td>Stainless chain, curves, sanitary construction</td><td>Bottles, cans, cartons</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">Live roller (belt-driven)</a></td><td>Motor size, roller diameter, heavy loads</td><td>Case transport, continuous flow</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">Powered roller (chain-driven)</a></td><td>Heavy-duty rollers, drives, chain guarding</td><td>Heavy cases, totes, pallet handling</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR (motor driven roller)</a></td><td>Zone count, zone controls, 24V DC power supplies</td><td><a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation</a>, zone control</td></tr>
<tr><td>Modular belt</td><td>Belt material, width, sanitary design</td><td><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food processing</a>, incline/decline</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Servo indexing belt</a></td><td>Servo drive and controller, precision belt, programming</td><td>Filling, labeling, pick-and-place</td></tr>
<tr><td>Spiral</td><td>Elevation, belt width, sanitary or freezer duty</td><td>Elevation change, cooling, freezing</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">Transfer (right-angle)</a></td><td>Lift mechanism, controls</td><td>Multi-directional routing, pallets</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">Pallet conveyor (chain)</a></td><td>Load capacity, structure, safety guarding</td><td>Heavy palletized loads, end-of-line</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The table assumes standard industrial construction. Sanitary, cleanroom, and hazardous-location ratings move any type upward.</p>
<h2 id="dims-heading">Length, Width &amp; Height</h2>
<p>Conveyor section cost grows with length, while drives and controls are shared across a run. Width matters more than it looks: wider conveyors need heavier frames, wider belts or longer rollers, and often larger motors.</p>
<p>Elevation adds cost through incline sections, supports, and larger drives to lift the load. Because belts have a maximum incline for each product, a given rise needs a minimum run length. Spiral conveyors save floor space for elevation changes but are among the most expensive types.</p>
<p><strong>Cost tip:</strong> keep conveyor runs short. Every foot of conveyor you do not need adds equipment, floor space, and maintenance. A layout with machines close together costs less than one with long transport sections. Ask the integrator to show the shortest layout that still gives the accumulation you need.</p>
<h2 id="materials-heading">Frame &amp; Belt Materials</h2>
<h3>Frame Material</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Painted carbon steel:</strong> standard industrial construction and the lowest-cost option. Suits dry environments handling cases, cartons, and totes. Not suited to washdown or food contact.</li>
<li><strong>Aluminum:</strong> light, corrosion-resistant, often extruded profiles with T-slots for modular mounting. Costs more than painted steel and less than stainless.</li>
<li><strong>Stainless steel:</strong> the norm for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary food-grade</a> and pharmaceutical conveyors. 304 is common in food plants. 316 costs more and resists salt, acids, and aggressive cleaning chemicals better. Stainless is a large step up from painted carbon steel for the same conveyor.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Belt Material</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>PVC:</strong> general-purpose belt for dry applications. Check food-contact rating if it matters.</li>
<li><strong>Polyurethane:</strong> food-contact formulations are available, often in white or blue for visual contamination checks. Common on <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food conveyors</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Modular plastic:</strong> interlocking modules. Damaged sections can be replaced without replacing the whole belt. Higher initial cost than basic flat belts.</li>
<li><strong>Silicone:</strong> high-temperature and release properties for bakery uses. A specialty belt that costs more and wears faster than polyurethane in many uses.</li>
<li><strong>Wire mesh:</strong> metal belts for heat, cooling, freezing, or heavy loads.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="motors-heading">Motors &amp; Drives</h2>
<p>From lowest to highest equipment cost:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>No motor (gravity):</strong> product moves by gravity or by hand. Lowest equipment and energy cost.</li>
<li><strong>Fixed-speed AC gearmotor:</strong> simple and reliable. Speed changes need a sprocket or pulley change.</li>
<li><strong>VFD-controlled AC gearmotor:</strong> adjustable speed, soft start and stop, and PLC speed control. The drive is an added cost over the motor. Standard on most <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems</a>.</li>
<li><strong>MDR motorized rollers:</strong> a low-voltage DC motor in each zone. Higher cost per zone, but zones run only when they have product to move.</li>
<li><strong>Servo drives:</strong> precise position, speed, and acceleration control for <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing conveyors</a>. Servo motor, drive, and programming cost well above a VFD and gearmotor. Worth it only when the application needs precise positioning or synchronization.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Washdown motors</strong> with sealed stainless or coated housings and high IP ratings cost more than standard enclosed motors. They are needed wherever conveyors are washed down daily with pressurized water or chemicals.</p>
<h2 id="controls-heading">Controls &amp; Accumulation</h2>
<p>Controls are often the most underestimated line item. The range is wide:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Manual start and stop:</strong> pushbutton station, motor starter, e-stop. No sensors and no PLC.</li>
<li><strong>Basic sensor logic:</strong> photoeyes with relay logic or a small PLC. Fine for standalone sections not tied to other machines.</li>
<li><strong>PLC-integrated:</strong> PLC with HMI controlling multiple sections, VFDs, sensors, interlocks, and diagnostics, plus programming, panel build, wiring, and commissioning.</li>
<li><strong>Full automation:</strong> servo motion, vision, <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject control</a>, recipes, data logging, OEE reporting, and plant network connections, with extensive programming and integration testing. See the <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems guide</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Accumulation adds cost in proportion to zone count. Each zero-pressure zone typically adds a sensor, a zone control, and a drive or clutch. Fewer, longer zones cost less but give less control over product spacing.</p>
<p><strong>Critical check:</strong> when comparing quotes, confirm what "controls" includes. One supplier may quote a motor starter and call it controls. Another may quote a full PLC package. This scope difference often explains most of the gap between quotes for the same conveyor.</p>
<h2 id="env-heading">Sanitation &amp; Environment Rating</h2>
<p>The operating environment raises the cost of any conveyor type:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Standard industrial (baseline):</strong> dry, room temperature, non-corrosive. Painted steel, standard motors and bearings.</li>
<li><strong>Washdown:</strong> stainless frames, washdown-rated motors and sensors, sealed bearings, food-contact belting, and open frames that drain and can be inspected. Built for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary conveyor</a> duty.</li>
<li><strong>Cleanroom:</strong> low-particle designs with enclosed drives, smooth surfaces, few exposed fasteners, and special lubricants, built to the cleanliness class of the room.</li>
<li><strong>Hazardous location:</strong> explosion-proof motors, sealed electrical enclosures, and intrinsically safe sensors for explosive dust or gas areas, with third-party certification. Usually the most expensive rating.</li>
<li><strong>Freezer or cold room:</strong> cold-rated lubricants, materials that tolerate condensation cycles, and motors rated for cold starts.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="guarding-heading">Guarding &amp; Safety</h2>
<p>Guarding is a required scope item, not an option. OSHA <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.212" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">29 CFR 1910.212</a> requires guarding against hazards such as ingoing nip points and rotating parts. <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.219" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">29 CFR 1910.219</a> covers drive shafts, pulleys, chains, and sprockets. Quotes differ in how much guarding they include, whether access doors are interlocked, and whether pull-cord e-stops are standard or optional. Guarding added after installation usually costs more than guarding designed in. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-safety/">conveyor safety guide</a> covers the requirements.</p>
<h2 id="install-heading">Installation &amp; Integration</h2>
<p>Installation cost depends on site conditions and integration scope:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mechanical installation:</strong> unloading, assembling, leveling, anchoring, and connecting sections. Bolt-together standard conveyors install faster than welded custom frames. Plan for rigging if sections exceed forklift capacity.</li>
<li><strong>Electrical installation:</strong> power and control wiring, motor and sensor connections, and panels. Scope grows with motor and sensor count and the distance to the power source.</li>
<li><strong>Controls commissioning:</strong> PLC programming, sensor setup, VFD parameters, and integration testing with other machines. Usually needs the supplier's controls engineer on site. Ask how many days are included.</li>
<li><strong>Structural supports:</strong> floor supports, overhead supports, mezzanine or wall penetrations. Runs above equipment or through walls add engineering and lead time.</li>
<li><strong>Removal of existing equipment:</strong> demolition and disposal, and abatement if old equipment has hazardous coatings or materials.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Cost tip:</strong> get installation prices from the conveyor supplier and from independent millwright and electrical contractors. The supplier knows the equipment. Independent crews may cost less but need time to learn it. On complex lines, a common split is supplier commissioning with an independent crew for mechanical and electrical work.</p>
<h2 id="tco-heading">Total Cost of Ownership</h2>
<p>Purchase price is only part of lifetime cost. Energy, wear parts, maintenance labor, and downtime add up over the life of the conveyor:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Energy:</strong> conveyors are constant torque loads, so power falls roughly in proportion to speed, according to the <a href="https://www.energy.gov/sites/prod/files/2014/04/f15/motor_tip_sheet11.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Department of Energy's adjustable speed drive tip sheet</a>. The larger savings come from not running empty sections, which is why <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> that run only occupied zones can use less energy than continuously running conveyors. Ask suppliers for an energy estimate at your duty cycle.</li>
<li><strong>Wear parts:</strong> belts, rollers, bearings, chains, and sprockets wear out. Replacement frequency depends on product abrasion, belt material, and speed. <a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">Modular plastic belts</a> cost more up front but allow section replacement.</li>
<li><strong>Maintenance labor:</strong> belt tracking, lubrication, roller inspection, and sensor alignment. MDR conveyors have no drive chains or line-shaft belts to adjust.</li>
<li><strong>Downtime:</strong> a conveyor failure that stops a packaging line costs the lost production. Features such as quick belt change and modular zone replacement cost more up front but shorten stops.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="questions-heading">Questions to Ask Manufacturers</h2>
<p>Use these questions when requesting and comparing quotes. They expose scope differences between suppliers:</p>
<h3>Scope &amp; Specification</h3>
<ol>
<li>What is included in "controls": motor starter only, or PLC, HMI, sensors, and VFDs?</li>
<li>What frame material is quoted: carbon steel, aluminum, or stainless? Which grade of stainless (304 or 316)?</li>
<li>What motor type and efficiency class is included? Does it meet current <a href="https://www.energy.gov/sites/default/files/2023-05/em-ecs-dfr.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DOE electric motor standards</a> where they apply?</li>
<li>Are sensors, mounting hardware, and wiring included, or customer-supplied?</li>
<li>What belt material is quoted, and what belt life do you expect at my operating conditions?</li>
</ol>
<h3>Lead Time &amp; Delivery</h3>
<ol start="6">
<li>What is the lead time from order to ship? Does it include the control panel build?</li>
<li>How will it ship: crated freight, flatbed, or delivered and installed?</li>
<li>Is pre-assembly available? Pre-assembled sections reduce on-site installation time.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Installation &amp; Commissioning</h3>
<ol start="9">
<li>What installation is included: mechanical only, or electrical and controls commissioning too?</li>
<li>How many days of on-site commissioning are included?</li>
<li>Who provides operator and maintenance training, and how many hours are included?</li>
</ol>
<h3>Warranty &amp; Support</h3>
<ol start="12">
<li>What is the warranty period, and does it cover parts only or parts and labor?</li>
<li>Which wear parts are excluded from warranty (belts, rollers, bearings)?</li>
<li>What wear-part replacement cost should I expect per year at my operating conditions?</li>
<li>What is the response time for warranty service: phone support hours and on-site response?</li>
</ol>
<h3>Future Expansion</h3>
<ol start="16">
<li>Can the system be extended later with longer runs or more sections?</li>
<li>Does the PLC have spare I/O for future sensors, motors, or integration?</li>
<li>Can the VFDs and motors handle higher speeds or loads if throughput rises?</li>
</ol>
<SpecChecklist id="spec-checklist" title="Quote request checklist" items={[
  ["Product data", "Product types, dimensions, weights, and bottom condition (flat case, tray, bottle, bag), including the smallest and largest product the conveyor must handle."],
  ["Rate and pitch", "Required products per minute or cases per minute, product spacing, and whether products arrive touching or gapped."],
  ["Layout", "Plan drawing with conveyor path, lengths, curves, elevation changes, transfer points, and fixed obstacles such as columns, doors, and aisles."],
  ["Interfaces", "Upstream and downstream machines, their infeed and discharge heights, and who is responsible for each transfer."],
  ["Accumulation", "Where product must accumulate, whether zero-pressure accumulation is required, and how long upstream machines must keep running during a downstream stop."],
  ["Frame and belt", "Frame material and stainless grade, belt or chain type, and whether belt materials must be rated for food contact."],
  ["Environment and sanitation", "Dry, washdown, cleanroom, freezer, or hazardous location, the cleaning chemicals used, and any sanitary design requirements."],
  ["Utilities", "Available voltage and phase, compressed air if needed, and the location of power and air drops."],
  ["Controls scope", "Starters only or PLC and HMI, PLC platform and network protocol to match plant standards, VFDs, sensors, and who writes the program."],
  ["Guarding and safety", "Guarding of drives and nip points, e-stop and pull-cord layout, interlocked access, and the safety standards the design must meet, such as ASME B20.1 and NFPA 79."],
  ["Installation and commissioning", "Who installs mechanical and electrical work, the number of commissioning days, training hours, and site access limits such as shutdown windows."],
  ["Acceptance test", "How the system will be accepted: the products, rate, run time, and jam criteria for the factory and site acceptance tests."]
]} />
